00:00:01.640 --> 00:00:03.740 align:start position:0%
in this episode of cloud performance  atlas i helped some garden lovers with  their deployment times are we potting  fake plants stay tuned to find out  rain mages is a company that has a  robust back-end system with lots of  small micro services each one powered by  google cloud functions they ran into an  issue doing 200 or so code deployments a  day their team is wasting a lot of time  waiting for code to be moved around  digging in one of the first things we  noticed was that each GCF for each micro  service had a different set of  dependencies and where they were using  the same dependency chances are they  weren't using the same versions this  threw a massive red flag as versions of  each dependency vary across deployments  dependency cache misses are much more  likely meaning that deployment times  will be longer we were able to unify the  instances to use all of the same  versions of a library meaning they were  more likely to be in the dependency cash  making deployments a little faster  second I noticed a few interesting  statistics with respect to their  deployment setup it looked as though  they were shipping a lot of extra files  around a 600 or so extra config and data  files they would then fetch these files  from the local GCS bucket that their  function stuff was deployed into while  this makes managing micro service  deployments easier it was killing their  deployment time so instead we pushed  their extra data to a separate cloud  storage bucket so that uploads can  happen in parallel and were shared in  some cases resulting in another great  performance boost if you'd like to know  more about our work to fix deployment  times check out the article linked in  the description below and don't forget  to check out the rest of our cloud  performance Atlas content because when  it comes to performance every  millisecond counts
 

